{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPkDYRAm1jMyqazbAjrp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I hit a hard blocker before I could do the research: **WebSearch was denied permission**, so I have not verified a single fact or citation in this session. Everything below is from model memory.\n\nFor a piece running as-is under your byline tonight, I won't hand you fabricated citations — invented author/title/year strings are the specific failure mode that gets a correction printed. So here's what I can honestly give you: the factual skeleton with confidence marked, plus the exact documents to pull.\n\n## What I'm confident about\n\n**The event.** The Oaks Colliery at Hoyle Mill, in the parish of Ardsley near Barnsley, West Riding of Yorkshire, working the Barnsley Thick Seam. First explosion Wednesday 12 December 1866, early afternoon (~1:20pm). It was the worst mining disaster in England, and the worst in Britain until Senghenydd in 1913 (439 dead). The same pit had suffered an earlier explosion in 1847 that killed 73.\n\n**The successive explosions.** A second explosion the following morning, Thursday 13 December, killed the rescue party that had gone down overnight. Further explosions continued over subsequent days, which is why the pit was sealed and the bodies left underground; recovery ran for months, into 1867 and beyond, and many of the dead were never identified. A mass grave and memorial are at Christ Church, Ardsley.\n\n**The rescue parties.** Parkin Jeffcock, a mining engineer, went down and was killed in the 13 December explosion — he is the name most associated with the rescue effort. Samuel Brown was found alive and brought up on 14 December, the only man to come out of the mine alive after the second explosion.\n\n## Where the numbers are genuinely contested\n\nThe total is usually given as **361**. You will also see 360 and, in older accounts, higher figures. That spread is real and worth a clause in your piece: the number underground was never certain, bodies were unrecoverable for months, and some injured died later.\n\nThe rescue-party toll is usually given as **27**. Subtracting leaves ~334 for the first explosion, but sources also print 340 for that figure — which doesn't reconcile. **Do not print a breakdown until you've checked one authoritative roll.**\n\n## What I will not state\n\n**The inquest verdict.** I recall it returning accidental death with no individual blamed, plus a jury rider on ventilation and lamps — but I am not confident of the wording, the coroner's name, or the date it concluded. This is exactly the fact you asked for and exactly the one I'd be guessing at. Leave it blank until sourced.\n\n## Pull these — they're the right documents\n\n- **Report of the Inspectors of Mines for 1866**, HMSO 1867, in the Parliamentary Papers. The Yorkshire inspector was Frank Nicholas Wardell. This is the primary source for cause and toll.\n- **Durham Mining Museum** (durhamminingmuseum.org.uk) — hosts a full roll of the Oaks dead and transcribed inspectors' reports. Best free path to a citable list.\n- **Coal Mining History Resource Centre**, Ian Winstanley's national database of mining deaths.\n- **Helen and Baron Duckham, *Great Pit Disasters: Great Britain 1700 to the Present Day*, David & Charles, 1973** — the one secondary book I'm reasonably confident of; verify before printing.\n- Contemporary press: *Illustrated London News* and *The Times*, late December 1866; *Barnsley Chronicle* for local detail. I can't give you dates or pages.\n\n---\n\nIf you grant WebSearch, I can verify all of this and get you real citations with page references — probably 20–30 minutes, which should still make tonight. Say the word and I'll run it, then publish the finished sheet as a page you can send your editor. Otherwise, the above is a research map, not a publishable draft."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":nul